On Thu, 27 Jan 2005 jasonnorthrup@yahoo.com wrote:

>Philipp Lucas wrote:
>> On Sun, 9 Jan 2005 jasonnorthrup@yahoo.com wrote:
>>> for realism, up staircases could be generated with one square of wall
>>> NSEW adjacent to them, though it might present a problem if this
>>> blocked a path, or made it necessary to travel diagonally along a path.
>> In NetHack, "realism" and "staircases" (or any kind of vertical
>> connectivity) do not match. Introducing such a block (which would
>> represent the space on which the stair ends on the upper level, I presume)
>> would only exemplify this further, because it would be another connection
>> of sequential levels, which NetHack does not really model.
>Then why is there a one-to-one correlation of down staircases to
>up-staircases

The connection through a staircase is the reason that I wrote "another
connection". Currently, staircases (and ladders) are the only physical
ways which connect levels at precise points. Having another essentially
random square which corresponds by its intended meaning to exactly one
spot on another level, would lead to two possible implementations:

  - Either make it so that this spot also works as a direct connection
    (when making a hole in the upper part). Then why stop here, why
    not define 1-to-1 correspondences for all squares of sequential
    levels?

  - Or let the square behave like any other square, in which case its
    whole existance is pointless.

